<p>In 1806 a nineteen-year-old Serb walked into the gymnasium at Sremski Karlovci, on the Austrian side of the border, hoping to enrol. He was turned away. The school did not take students his age, and he had no Latin, which was where a real education started in that part of the world. He had already been refused help by the most famous scholar of his people. He would spend most of his life in rented rooms in Vienna, short of money, and he would die with his life's work still unaccepted in the country it was for.</p>

<p>His name was <strong>Vuk Stefanović Karadžić</strong>. In the space of four decades he standardised a literary language on the speech of villagers nobody consulted, produced the first serious Serbian dictionary, and cut the Serbian Cyrillic alphabet down to <strong>thirty letters with one sound each</strong>, giving his language one of the most consistent spelling systems in Europe. Schoolchildren in Serbia still learn to read it in weeks.</p>

<h2>Tršić, 1787: A Village Where Almost Nobody Could Read</h2>

<p>Vuk was born on 6 November 1787 in Tršić, a village near Loznica, then inside the Ottoman Empire, to a family of Serbian peasants. He was small, sickly and, after an illness in childhood, permanently lame — the paralysis of his left lower leg stayed with him all his life, and he called it his withered leg and walked with a stick. What he had instead of health was a relative: Jevta Savić Čotrić, the only literate man anywhere near the village, who taught him his letters. Later he was sent to the old Tronoša monastery, where he learned calligraphy with a reed instead of a pen and gunpowder dissolved in water instead of ink, writing on cartridge paper because there was nothing better.</p>

<p>It did not last. At the monastery he spent more time pasturing livestock than studying, and his father eventually brought him home. Regular schooling barely existed in the region; when his father finally agreed he could go to Austria to study, the war intervened.</p>

<h2>Refused, Patronised, and Put to Work</h2>

<p>The First Serbian Uprising against Ottoman rule began in 1804, and Vuk's adult life began inside it. His attempt at the Karlovci gymnasium failed. He then spent a few months in Petrinja learning Latin and German — his only real classroom experience. When he reached liberated Belgrade he asked <strong>Dositej Obradović</strong>, the great Enlightenment figure of Serbian letters, to support his studies; Obradović dismissed him.</p>

<p>So he made himself useful instead of educated. Because he could write, he became a <strong>scribe</strong> — to the voivode Jakov Nenadović, later as a customs officer, and later as a teacher in the villages. When the Belgrade Higher School opened in 1808, he became one of its first students. It was, by any standard, a scrapheap education: a literate cousin, a monastery, three months of Latin, and a war that turned him into a clerk.</p>

<p>In 1813 the uprising collapsed. Most of the leaders fled east to Russia. Vuk went west to Vienna.</p>

<h2>Vienna: The Only Place the Work Could Be Printed</h2>

<p>Vienna is where the second half of his education happened, and it is where he found the person who made the first half matter. <strong>Jernej Kopitar</strong>, a Slovene who was both a Slavist and the Habsburg censor for Slavic books, recognised what the lame scribe from Tršić had: an ear trained on the spoken language of ordinary people, and none of the assumptions a university would have given him.</p>

<p>Kopitar pushed him in one direction. Collect the songs. Write the grammar of how people actually speak. Build a dictionary. And through Kopitar, Vuk met the work of the German philologist Johann Christoph Adelung, whose principle became the motto of everything he did afterwards:</p>

<blockquote>Write as you speak, and read as it is written. — <strong>Johann Christoph Adelung, adopted by Vuk as the founding rule of Serbian orthography</strong></blockquote>

<p>In Vienna he also taught himself the languages a philologist needed: German, then Russian, and later French, Italian and Greek. Not in a seminar. In the same years he was writing his own grammar, and the title he gave it was a manifesto in itself: <em>Grammar of the Serbian Language According to the Speech of the Common People</em>, published in 1814.</p>

<h2>The Problem: Thirty Sounds, Forty-Three Letters</h2>

<p>Serbian as Vuk heard it had about thirty distinct sounds. The Cyrillic alphabet he had learned to write had more than forty letters, and it had no single letters at all for six of the sounds people actually used: <em>lj</em>, <em>nj</em>, <em>đ</em>, <em>ć</em>, <em>dž</em> and <em>j</em>. Worse, the learned language of the church and the schools was a Slavonic-Serbian hybrid that ordinary Serbs could not understand and nobody spoke.</p>

<p>Vuk's work built on an earlier attempt by Sava Mrkalj, and he pushed the logic all the way: he removed every letter that had no sound in the vernacular, and created or borrowed letters for the six sounds that had none. Two were made by ligating letters with the soft sign (Љ, Њ). One was taken from a design by Lukijan Mušicki, one from old Serbian manuscripts, one from old Romanian manuscripts. The last, <strong>Ј</strong>, was lifted straight from the Latin alphabet — and that single letter caused more outrage than all the rest combined, because it looked like a step toward Rome.</p>

<p>He made one error and corrected it himself. He first omitted <strong>Х</strong>, believing the sound did not exist in the vernacular, then reinstated it in 1836 after finding it in use in Montenegro, Bosnia and Dubrovnik. The result was an alphabet of thirty letters in which each sound has exactly one letter and each letter exactly one sound.</p>

<h2>1818: A Dictionary Written from Villagers' Mouths</h2>

<p>Four years after the grammar, Vuk published the <em>Srpski rječnik</em> — a Serbian dictionary with German and Latin glosses containing <strong>26,270 words</strong>, along with a great deal of ethnography, proverbs and folklore. Its second edition in 1852 ran to around <strong>47,000 words</strong> and is still regarded as a classic.</p>

<p>The method is what makes it interesting to anyone who has ever tried to build a vocabulary deck. Vuk did not compile his word list from books. He travelled — through Serbia, Bosnia, Croatia, Montenegro, the Bay of Kotor — and collected what people said. He recruited correspondents who sent him material by post for years at a time, among them the priest Vuk Popović and the aspiring writer Vuk Vrčević, who between them fed him songs, tales and lexical material from the coast for decades. It was a distributed data-collection project run from a rented room in Vienna, twenty years before anyone had a word for it.</p>

<p>Between 1814 and 1833 he published collections of Serbian folk poetry — <em>Srpske narodne pjesme</em>, four volumes starting in 1823 — that did what no dictionary could: they proved to Europe that the "vulgar" speech of peasants could carry literature. Jacob Grimm championed him. Goethe read the songs with admiration. The international reputation Vuk could never win at home came from foreign scholars first.</p>

<h2>The Fight He Nearly Lost</h2>

<p>For more than thirty years, Vuk was the enemy. The Serbian Orthodox Church, led by Metropolitan Stefan Stratimirović of Karlovci, saw his reform as an attack on the sanctity of Church Slavonic, and some opponents spread the charge that he was trying to <strong>Catholicise the Serbs</strong> by introducing the Latin letter <em>j</em>. The conservative intellectuals — the learned men of the day, led by Jovan Hadžić — called the vernacular rustic and unfit for scholarship. The authorities in the Principality of Serbia, under church influence, long forbade the printing of books in his orthography.</p>

<p>What he was accused of is worth listing, because it is exactly what he had done: he had abandoned the Church Slavonic foundation, he had put "indecent" vernacular words into a dictionary, and he had built a literary language on the speech of one ordinary dialect region — Eastern Herzegovina — instead of the archaic bookish register of the elite.</p>

<p>He answered with polemics, and with books. The decisive year came in <strong>1847</strong>, when four things appeared almost together: Vuk's own translation of the New Testament into the reformed Serbian language; Njegoš's epic <em>The Mountain Wreath</em>; the poetry of Branko Radičević; and Đuro Daničić's philological demolition of the old spelling, which argued his opponents into irrelevance. Within a generation the vernacular had won in literature. Official adoption took longer: the Principality of Serbia made Vuk's orthography the state's in <strong>1868</strong>, four years after his death.</p>

<h2>Buried Next to the Man Who Dismissed Him</h2>

<p>Vuk died in Vienna on 7 February 1864, aged 76, still working, still short of money, with his alphabet pending. In 1897 his remains were brought to Belgrade and buried with state honours in front of St Michael's Cathedral — in the grave next to Dositej Obradović, the famous scholar who had turned away an unknown young man from Belgrade eighty years earlier. His daughter Mina became a painter and translator; his son Dimitrije a soldier. His alphabet became the alphabet, and the reform he never saw accepted is now simply how Serbian is written.</p>

<h2>What a Learner Can Take from Vuk</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Learn the language where it lives.</strong> Vuk's dictionary came from conversations, songs and marketplaces, not from a library of previous dictionaries. Whatever your target language, the register you want is the spoken one, and no syllabus contains all of it.</li>
  <li><strong>Consistency is a design decision, and you can make it too.</strong> His alphabet works because he insisted on one sound per letter and cut anything that did not earn its place. You can do the same with your notes and flashcards: one card, one fact, one question; delete the ones with two answers.</li>
  <li><strong>Refused entrance is information, not a verdict.</strong> He was told he was too old and too late at nineteen. He learned Latin, German, Russian, French, Italian and Greek from the age of nineteen onward, by himself, while running a national literary project. The Karlovci gymnasium is not remembered.</li>
  <li><strong>Recruit correspondents.</strong> Vuk collected across a whole region by asking people to send him what they heard. A study partner, a language exchange, a group chat where everybody posts one sentence a day — that is the same machine, at the same cost.</li>
  <li><strong>Do not judge your work by its reception.</strong> The church called him a traitor to the faith; the learned men called him vulgar; his alphabet became official four years after he died. He kept publishing anyway, in a rented room, for fifty years.</li>
</ul>

<p>There is a strange symmetry between his two great acts. He saved the songs of people who could not write, and he gave those same people an alphabet they could learn in a season. Both were arguments for the same idea — that the speech of ordinary people is a legitimate foundation for a literature, and that a writing system should serve the reader rather than the scholar. It took a village boy who never got the education he wanted to make that case stick.</p>

